WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:01.980
All right, everyone...

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:05.980
Only 40 minutes remain in the
first match of the Monaco Cup finals.

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:10.200
Make as delicious a bread as possible, quickly, quickly!

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:12.410
What should we do?

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:14.910
There’s only 40 minutes left...

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:17.290
Azuma still hasn’t come back yet...

00:00:17.290 --> 00:00:17.950
O-Oh yeah,

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:21.920
while making your <i>Lupan</i>s, you
studied breads from all over the world, right?

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:25.340
Didn’t you come across the world’s
fastest-baking bread or something?

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:29.470
There was a book with a recipe to make bread quickly and mechanically,

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:30.970
at the expense of taste,

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:32.970
but I had no interest in it,
so I threw it away.

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:34.890
You moron!!

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:36.200
When you’re studying,

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:38.970
sometimes you have to suck it up and study topics you have no interest in!

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:40.560
Oh?

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:43.150
You have the nerve to preach to me?

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:46.820
Then, I’ll just fix that rotten mouth of
yours so that it can never speak again!

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:48.490
Fix yourself down there!

00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:50.070
Y-Y-You’re going to cut me?!

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:51.860
You’re going to cut me, you Neanderthal!

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:53.120
Yes.

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:55.780
Prepare yourself.

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:57.990
Sorry for the wait, everyone.

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:03.170
We’ll be fine now that we have this.

00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:04.460
Azuma-kun!

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:07.210
Really, you’re the only one we can always rely on.

00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:10.920
Bandanna Boy here’s all show,
and can’t be relied on at all.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:11.880
Baldy...

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:15.920
I wonder what secret tool Azuma-kun brought...

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:17.680
Wow, this is...

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:20.190
Isn’t this just a regular microwave?!

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:21.180
<b>Ping~</b>

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:22.100
That’s right.

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:23.310
You moron!!

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:24.350
This isn’t dinner!

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:28.150
I’ve never even heard of making bread by simply warming it up with a “ping” of the microwave.

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:33.650
We don’t have a frozen bread
to warm up in the first place...

00:01:33.650 --> 00:01:35.820
What are you talking about, Kawachi?

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:37.910
We’re not warming anything up.

00:01:37.910 --> 00:01:40.660
We’re going to make a new bread, using this microwave.

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:42.410
Huh?

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:44.160
Microwave-bread.

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:47.420
<i>Japan</i> #3.

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:50.000
Microwave-bread?

00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:54.130
There’s English bread, German bread, and French bread,

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:56.300
but Japan’s bread, <i>Japan</i>, does not exist.

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:58.350
In that case, there’s no choice but to create it.

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:02.000
This story is a serious, biographical ballad of a boy who possesses Solar Hands,

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:06.770
Azuma Kazuma, who will create a Japanese bread made by and for the Japanese people,

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:11.200
which can be presented to the world proudly.

00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:44.330
A speed contest!!

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:46.040
<i>Ping</i> it with that!

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:53.170
We’re English, and we love tea.

00:03:53.170 --> 00:03:55.760
Occasionally, we hang out at pubs, too.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:58.260
You can’t buy our bread with Euros.

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:00.800
We add malt vinegar to everything.

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:03.130
Fish ’n’ chips.

00:04:03.130 --> 00:04:06.890
Getting to the point, we’re just fast!

00:04:09.770 --> 00:04:11.560
That was really fast...

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:14.150
It’s only been 20 minutes.

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:16.150
That was fast...

00:04:16.150 --> 00:04:18.280
You guys are the first to finish.

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:19.780
C’mon, eat.

00:04:19.780 --> 00:04:21.650
You multiplying-clown bastard.

00:04:24.910 --> 00:04:26.660
This English bread...

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:31.040
is disqualified!

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:32.960
No!

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:37.800
D-Disqualified, just like that?!

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:40.800
How can you disqualify us by
just looking at and touching it?

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:45.310
I shall say it as many times as necessary...

00:04:45.310 --> 00:04:47.050
It’s because I’m a clown!!

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:48.430
Oh, no!

00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:50.010
Everyone’s happy in London Tower!

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:52.900
Once you become a world-level clown,

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:58.270
you have the ability to see the skin texture of each and every one of your 10,000 customers.

00:04:58.650 --> 00:04:59.490
Oh, no!

00:04:59.490 --> 00:05:01.280
Fun in London Tower!

00:05:01.570 --> 00:05:04.280
In order to make that bread rise fast,

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:07.380
you added more than the
required amount of yeast, right?

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:11.290
Even without using a microscope,

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:16.290
my eyes can see the shapes of the
yeast squirming sadly against each other.

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:19.960
Moreover,

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:22.430
when cut, this bread is extremely coarse!

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:27.050
Regardless of how fast you made it,
I cannot possibly pass such a bread!

00:05:32.140 --> 00:05:36.560
I guess England really is
helpless when America isn’t around...

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:39.570
We, the Americans, finished second.

00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:43.280
It’s dinner bread like that of the English. However,
it’s not just one, but two levels above theirs.

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:47.780
Hmm...

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:49.780
It looks like...

00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:53.790
you used the new Dragon Hook, didn’t you?

00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:55.670
That’s right.

00:05:56.290 --> 00:06:01.790
We equipped a mixer that has twice the
normal horsepower with the Dragon Hook.

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:06.970
We mixed the dough at a speed impossible for humans,

00:06:06.970 --> 00:06:10.180
reducing the time required for the
dough to rise to a third of the usual.

00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:16.020
This creates a dough that’s so
light it’s called “Balloon Bread.”

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:20.070
In other words, the production
time’s a third of the normal time.

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:22.820
This is bread made by the
Super Strong Mixing Technique.

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:24.740
I don’t even need to eat it.

00:06:24.740 --> 00:06:25.780
You pass.

00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:27.950
Good job, Team America.

00:06:27.950 --> 00:06:29.990
You’re the first to pass.

00:06:29.990 --> 00:06:31.280
All right!

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:36.250
A Dragon Hook on a super-strong mixer?

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:39.920
To think that they have the latest
technology at their fingertips like that...

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:43.040
As expected of the elite selected by St. Pierre’s owner.

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:45.510
These American representatives...

00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:47.550
They’re going to be tough to beat.

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:50.800
A-And, compared to that...

00:06:53.770 --> 00:06:56.770
we’re using something like a microwave...?

00:06:57.690 --> 00:06:59.860
All right, then, time for the second fermentation...

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:01.150
Hey, Azuma...

00:07:01.150 --> 00:07:01.810
Yeah?

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:05.150
It may not be my place to say this,

00:07:05.150 --> 00:07:07.320
but shouldn’t we be using more advanced technology,

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:10.320
like the Dragon Hook, rather than microwaves?

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:12.080
Captain Cook?

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:13.040
Who’s that?

00:07:13.410 --> 00:07:14.290
Umm...

00:07:14.290 --> 00:07:17.170
I believe he’s the guy who discovered Hawaii...

00:07:17.170 --> 00:07:18.720
That’s not it!!!

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:20.290
I can’t take this...

00:07:21.460 --> 00:07:24.460
If you worry so much,
you’ll really go bald,

00:07:24.460 --> 00:07:25.470
Baldy.

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:26.130
What was that?!

00:07:26.130 --> 00:07:28.010
The second to pass is our very own France.

00:07:28.930 --> 00:07:32.100
This is a truly splendid Irish soda bread.

00:07:35.930 --> 00:07:40.990
Usually, you use only baking
powder in Irish soda bread to make it rise.

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:47.020
But, using yeast gives it an ever richer taste.

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:49.530
But of course.

00:07:49.530 --> 00:07:51.240
N-No way...

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:53.620
France passed already...

00:07:53.620 --> 00:07:55.530
No, it isn’t just France.

00:07:56.080 --> 00:07:59.080
The third to pass is Vietnam.

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:00.710
China is fourth.

00:08:00.710 --> 00:08:02.340
Egypt is fifth.

00:08:02.340 --> 00:08:03.840
Korea is sixth.

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:06.350
Denmark is seventh!

00:08:08.300 --> 00:08:10.550
In these remaining five minutes,

00:08:10.550 --> 00:08:13.550
the number of teams that have
passed is successively increasing.

00:08:15.220 --> 00:08:17.980
Only the best eight teams will be able to pass!

00:08:17.980 --> 00:08:20.560
There’s only one slot left!

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:22.310
Azuma!!

00:08:24.440 --> 00:08:26.520
It’s done!

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:30.490
Azuma!

00:08:30.490 --> 00:08:32.240
Pass the bread to me!

00:08:32.530 --> 00:08:33.870
Kawachi!

00:08:33.870 --> 00:08:35.820
Pass!

00:08:40.210 --> 00:08:41.420
And, it’s a catch!

00:08:41.420 --> 00:08:42.880
Hot, hot, hot!

00:08:42.880 --> 00:08:44.290
Suwa-chii!

00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:56.480
Sorry about this.

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:01.980
Here you go, eat it while it’s hot.

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:04.980
This is Azuma’s <i>Japan</i> #3.

00:09:16.330 --> 00:09:20.080
Here you go, eat it while it’s hot.

00:09:20.370 --> 00:09:22.550
This is Azuma’s <i>Japan</i> #3.

00:09:23.580 --> 00:09:26.500
Is that bread really all right?

00:09:26.500 --> 00:09:28.380
Yeah, it should be.

00:09:28.380 --> 00:09:29.590
How do you know?

00:09:29.590 --> 00:09:32.510
Bread made using a microwave...

00:09:32.510 --> 00:09:34.800
Actually, even I’ve tried making it.

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:38.840
So, it was you who taught Kazuma to make that?

00:09:39.140 --> 00:09:40.980
No, not at all.

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:43.440
I used a book as reference while making it.

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:47.570
However, Azuma probably
came up with that idea by himself.

00:09:47.570 --> 00:09:50.820
As usual, his ability to conceptualize things never ceases to amaze me.

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:53.450
But, the outcome of the match depends on its taste.

00:09:53.450 --> 00:09:55.820
It doesn’t matter if it was
completed within the time limit...

00:09:55.820 --> 00:09:58.910
No, it’s pretty clear from the way that bread looks.

00:09:58.910 --> 00:10:00.830
There should be nothing to
criticize with regard to its taste.

00:10:00.830 --> 00:10:02.830
There’s no doubt that they’ll pass.

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:09.000
Exactly how do you make that
delicious a bread using a microwave?

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:10.090
Do you really want to know?

00:10:10.090 --> 00:10:11.340
Uh... yeah...

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:12.640
Is that so?

00:10:14.970 --> 00:10:16.340
After a long time,

00:10:16.340 --> 00:10:19.480
it seems that I have a real chance to shine!

00:10:21.890 --> 00:10:23.940
If my memory serves me right...

00:10:23.940 --> 00:10:28.890
First, add 75ml of milk and 8g of unsalted
butter to a large heat-resistant mixing bowl.

00:10:28.890 --> 00:10:29.190
On high for 30 seconds

00:10:29.190 --> 00:10:30.940
Microwave it on high for 30 seconds.

00:10:30.940 --> 00:10:32.030
Blend completely.

00:10:32.030 --> 00:10:34.410
Next, whisk until completely blended.

00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:37.370
Add salt, sugar, and bread flour.

00:10:37.370 --> 00:10:39.240
Mix well and add the remaining flour.

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:40.990
Mix well and put into microwave for 30 seconds.

00:10:43.410 --> 00:10:45.580
With this, the first fermentation is complete.

00:10:45.580 --> 00:10:47.500
Next, place it on the chopping board and divide it.

00:10:47.500 --> 00:10:50.460
Cover the dough with cooking paper and
a wet cloth, and leave it for 10 minutes.

00:10:50.460 --> 00:10:51.960
Press with your fingers, and put in
microwave for 30 seconds on low.

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:54.470
Sprinkle it with sesame seeds, cover with, the cooking paper and wet cloth, and let it rise.

00:10:54.470 --> 00:10:56.340
With this the second fermentation is complete!

00:10:56.340 --> 00:10:58.970
Next, just bake it at 200°C in the oven.

00:11:01.810 --> 00:11:03.890
With this, it’s complete!

00:11:03.890 --> 00:11:05.100
How was that?

00:11:05.100 --> 00:11:06.060
Did you get it?

00:11:07.270 --> 00:11:08.560
Hey, are you listening to me?

00:11:08.560 --> 00:11:12.530
I’m too worried about everyone to pay attention.

00:11:12.530 --> 00:11:14.570
Hey, are we really okay?

00:11:14.570 --> 00:11:15.820
Yeah.

00:11:15.820 --> 00:11:18.240
Both the recipe and the actual baking are perfect.

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:21.200
I, a judge at Pantasia, say so.

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:22.490
No mistake about it.

00:11:22.490 --> 00:11:24.580
Then, what’s that?

00:11:24.580 --> 00:11:25.580
Hmm?

00:11:29.960 --> 00:11:32.620
To tell you the truth,
I’m disappointed in you.

00:11:32.620 --> 00:11:34.090
Wh-What?

00:11:34.090 --> 00:11:38.730
This is going to turn into a tragic ending for you.

00:11:39.090 --> 00:11:42.340
So, be prepared, Japanese representatives.

00:11:42.340 --> 00:11:43.810
What does this mean?

00:11:43.810 --> 00:11:45.350
I... I don’t know.

00:11:45.350 --> 00:11:49.610
It’s true that this bread looks
pretty good, so I shall eat it.

00:11:49.610 --> 00:11:54.100
However, I don’t think it will change the outcome.

00:11:54.900 --> 00:11:56.030
But, why?

00:11:56.030 --> 00:11:58.320
Old– I mean, Mister Pierrot.

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:00.070
“Why,” you ask?

00:12:00.070 --> 00:12:01.540
That’s because...

00:12:04.580 --> 00:12:07.750
I really...

00:12:08.330 --> 00:12:11.330
hate microwaves!

00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:18.420
How can this be?!

00:12:18.420 --> 00:12:19.840
Th-This is ridiculous!

00:12:28.390 --> 00:12:32.700
I just have really bad
memories associated with microwaves.

00:12:33.150 --> 00:12:37.610
Therefore, the grading will be harsher than it usually is.

00:12:37.610 --> 00:12:39.070
Say your prayers.

00:12:39.740 --> 00:12:41.240
Azuma!

00:12:41.240 --> 00:12:43.240
That’s why I told you!

00:12:43.240 --> 00:12:47.370
We should be using the latest technology,
like a Dragon Hook, rather than microwaves.

00:12:47.370 --> 00:12:49.080
100-yen shop?

00:12:49.790 --> 00:12:51.710
Oh, booger it.

00:12:51.710 --> 00:12:53.580
Quit clowning around, clown.

00:12:54.090 --> 00:12:56.500
This competition is supposed to be a speed contest.

00:12:56.500 --> 00:13:00.090
We just have to make as delicious a
bread in as little time as possible, right?

00:13:00.470 --> 00:13:03.590
So, while you may hate microwaves,

00:13:03.590 --> 00:13:06.350
as long as we make a bread that
tastes good, shouldn’t we pass?

00:13:06.350 --> 00:13:08.560
That is true, but...

00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:11.640
isn’t there anything that you guys hate?!

00:13:11.640 --> 00:13:14.890
Are you able to accept everything in this world?!

00:13:14.890 --> 00:13:16.850
Can you love it all?!

00:13:17.650 --> 00:13:19.030
W-What does this mean?

00:13:19.030 --> 00:13:22.700
For example, if the topic turns to
something nasty during dinner time,

00:13:22.700 --> 00:13:24.410
no matter how tasty the food is...

00:13:24.410 --> 00:13:25.700
Uh, yeah...

00:13:25.700 --> 00:13:27.080
I guess I wouldn’t want to eat it...

00:13:27.990 --> 00:13:29.580
That’s a cowardly example.

00:13:29.580 --> 00:13:31.200
It’s all right.

00:13:31.910 --> 00:13:32.790
What was that?!

00:13:32.790 --> 00:13:34.380
You’d be able to eat it?

00:13:34.380 --> 00:13:35.670
That’s not it!

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:39.010
I believe in Mister Pierrot.

00:13:39.460 --> 00:13:41.880
<i>Japan</i> #3 is delicious.

00:13:41.880 --> 00:13:43.920
I’m sure he’ll pass us.

00:13:45.430 --> 00:13:48.430
I’m happy that you place your faith in me...

00:13:51.270 --> 00:13:54.320
But if you hate it, you hate it.

00:13:55.020 --> 00:13:57.440
Are you prepared,
Japanese representatives?

00:14:11.500 --> 00:14:12.910
That...

00:14:13.870 --> 00:14:16.970
happened in the plane on the way to Las Vegas.

00:14:19.710 --> 00:14:22.380
Bolneze, may I sit here?

00:14:22.920 --> 00:14:23.760
Boss...

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:24.840
Hey, hey.

00:14:24.840 --> 00:14:28.180
You’ll be the boss starting from
the performance in Las Vegas, right?

00:14:28.180 --> 00:14:30.850
So, just call me by name from now onwards.

00:14:32.600 --> 00:14:33.930
Very well.

00:14:33.930 --> 00:14:35.390
So, what is it, Don?

00:14:35.810 --> 00:14:39.480
We will now be going to perform in Las Vegas.

00:14:39.480 --> 00:14:41.110
As the boss,

00:14:41.110 --> 00:14:45.410
there’s one thing you must
remember while performing in America.

00:14:45.950 --> 00:14:48.400
Something I must remember?

00:14:48.400 --> 00:14:49.620
Yeah.

00:14:49.620 --> 00:14:53.040
As you know, America’s big on trials.

00:14:53.040 --> 00:14:55.920
Apparently, they have half of the world’s lawyers,

00:14:55.920 --> 00:14:59.130
an impressive 900,000 in number...

00:14:59.130 --> 00:15:02.920
18 million trials take place there every year.

00:15:02.920 --> 00:15:05.670
18... 18 million trials?!

00:15:05.670 --> 00:15:07.180
It’s an amazing number, right?

00:15:07.180 --> 00:15:08.220
Yeah.

00:15:08.220 --> 00:15:10.390
But, what about it?

00:15:10.970 --> 00:15:13.390
It doesn’t have anything to
do with us, as a circus troupe.

00:15:13.390 --> 00:15:14.570
Naive, aren’t you...

00:15:15.270 --> 00:15:16.810
Listen up, Bolneze.

00:15:16.810 --> 00:15:18.400
There’s a story that goes like this...

00:15:18.400 --> 00:15:20.400
Wait a moment!

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:24.520
What does this story have
to do with judging the bread?

00:15:24.820 --> 00:15:27.820
Please don’t interrupt at the good part.

00:15:27.820 --> 00:15:30.030
Things like that will affect my judgment.

00:15:30.030 --> 00:15:31.990
What?! You...

00:15:31.990 --> 00:15:34.660
Shut up and listen to Pierrot-sensei’s story.

00:15:34.660 --> 00:15:35.330
Come, come.

00:15:35.330 --> 00:15:37.040
Please continue with the story, Sensei.

00:15:40.290 --> 00:15:42.050
Listen up, Bolneze.

00:15:42.670 --> 00:15:44.540
There’s a story that goes like this...

00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:47.050
There, there.

00:15:47.050 --> 00:15:49.550
Just be patient for a few more minutes.

00:15:50.300 --> 00:15:52.300
Hey there, Marie-chan.

00:15:52.300 --> 00:15:53.800
Calm down.

00:15:54.430 --> 00:15:58.190
If you don’t let me dry you
off properly, you’ll catch a cold.

00:15:58.190 --> 00:15:59.690
Oh, that’s right!

00:15:59.690 --> 00:16:01.310
There’s a good way of doing this.

00:16:03.900 --> 00:16:07.540
If we use the microwave, you’ll be dry in a jiffy.

00:16:09.320 --> 00:16:12.670
But, if you do something like that...

00:16:15.370 --> 00:16:16.500
And then...

00:16:19.040 --> 00:16:22.170
Please forgive this ignorant old lady,

00:16:22.170 --> 00:16:25.850
who didn’t even know that you can’t dry cats in the microwave...

00:16:26.510 --> 00:16:28.430
There’s no need to blame yourself.

00:16:29.630 --> 00:16:30.920
Y-You are...?

00:16:32.180 --> 00:16:33.600
I’m Kid.

00:16:33.600 --> 00:16:34.890
A lawyer.

00:16:34.890 --> 00:16:37.430
I’ve come to help you avenge your cat.

00:16:40.440 --> 00:16:41.560
Ma’am...

00:16:41.560 --> 00:16:46.320
was there a warning on the microwave saying that you must not use it to dry animals?

00:16:46.320 --> 00:16:48.280
N-No.

00:16:48.280 --> 00:16:50.200
There was no such thing anywhere...

00:16:50.570 --> 00:16:53.820
If so, it wasn’t your fault that you
used the microwave to dry your cat.

00:16:55.490 --> 00:16:56.830
Th-That’s right!

00:16:56.830 --> 00:16:59.950
If there was a warning describing how to use it,

00:16:59.950 --> 00:17:02.700
I wouldn’t have dried Marie-chan.

00:17:02.700 --> 00:17:03.540
That’s right.

00:17:03.540 --> 00:17:08.210
It’s not unusual for an elderly person like yourself to think of drying her cat in the microwave.

00:17:08.210 --> 00:17:13.420
Therefore, it’s clearly the fault of the manufacturer for not adding a proper warning.

00:17:13.720 --> 00:17:14.760
That’s right!

00:17:14.760 --> 00:17:15.680
That’s right, isn’t it?

00:17:15.680 --> 00:17:16.810
That’s right.

00:17:16.810 --> 00:17:19.430
So, let’s sue that dastardly manufacturer, together.

00:17:19.810 --> 00:17:22.100
Let’s avenge Marie-chan’s death!

00:17:22.100 --> 00:17:23.320
Okay!

00:17:25.480 --> 00:17:29.780
So, the old lady sued the
manufacturer, and won the case.

00:17:29.780 --> 00:17:30.070
Yeah!
So, the old lady sued the
manufacturer, and won the case.

00:17:30.070 --> 00:17:32.360
She received a large sum as consolation money.

00:17:32.360 --> 00:17:34.360
I-Impossible!

00:17:34.700 --> 00:17:37.240
It really is a ridiculous story.

00:17:37.240 --> 00:17:39.870
But... that’s America.

00:17:39.870 --> 00:17:40.910
Listen carefully.

00:17:40.910 --> 00:17:46.880
Your cloning technique might be
dangerous for the weak-hearted and elderly.

00:17:47.460 --> 00:17:49.670
In order to keep from being sued,

00:17:49.670 --> 00:17:52.130
be sure to put up warning signs everywhere.

00:17:52.130 --> 00:17:54.180
I-I understand.

00:17:55.680 --> 00:17:59.510
Thus, we put up warnings all over the ring...

00:18:03.190 --> 00:18:06.520
and we were able to bring the
show to a safe and successful end.

00:18:06.730 --> 00:18:07.730
Huh?

00:18:07.730 --> 00:18:09.570
I don’t understand you.

00:18:09.570 --> 00:18:11.770
If it was a success, then isn’t it a good memory?

00:18:11.770 --> 00:18:14.700
It’s true that the performance was a great success.

00:18:14.700 --> 00:18:15.990
However...

00:18:15.990 --> 00:18:19.370
Every time that I cloned myself in Las Vegas,

00:18:19.370 --> 00:18:22.790
I was just worried about whether or
not there were people who might be sick,

00:18:22.790 --> 00:18:24.410
and so, I didn’t enjoy myself at all.

00:18:24.790 --> 00:18:27.000
This microwave-bread is the same.

00:18:27.380 --> 00:18:30.090
It’s really delicious, but...

00:18:30.090 --> 00:18:32.720
precisely because it’s so very delicious,

00:18:32.720 --> 00:18:36.390
all that I can think of is the microwave and the cat.

00:18:36.390 --> 00:18:38.420
I cannot enjoy eating it at all!

00:18:38.680 --> 00:18:40.560
Just wait a minute.

00:18:40.560 --> 00:18:42.770
That’s just you imagining things on your own!

00:18:42.770 --> 00:18:45.430
If it’s delicious, then just pass us!

00:18:45.430 --> 00:18:47.560
You are such troublesome people...

00:18:47.560 --> 00:18:48.900
Look who’s talking!

00:18:48.900 --> 00:18:50.520
I shall repeat myself as many times as you would like.

00:18:50.520 --> 00:18:52.570
But, the impression left by food is also very important.

00:18:52.570 --> 00:18:55.070
No matter how delicious this bread may be,

00:18:55.070 --> 00:18:58.490
because you used a microwave, which I detest,

00:18:58.490 --> 00:19:01.450
you have lost quite a few points.

00:19:01.910 --> 00:19:04.200
In other words, even before being tasted,

00:19:04.200 --> 00:19:09.080
your bread had lost all chances of ever passing!

00:19:09.080 --> 00:19:10.290
No way!

00:19:10.290 --> 00:19:11.460
That’s completely unreasonable.

00:19:11.460 --> 00:19:14.260
No, like Pierrot said...

00:19:14.260 --> 00:19:17.210
the impression left by food is very important.

00:19:17.210 --> 00:19:18.640
But...

00:19:18.640 --> 00:19:21.430
to blame them to this extent
on his personal preferences...

00:19:21.430 --> 00:19:23.220
There’s nothing more we can do.

00:19:24.100 --> 00:19:25.430
He’s kidding, right?

00:19:25.430 --> 00:19:27.430
Th-This can’t be...

00:19:27.690 --> 00:19:29.940
I’m sorry, Japanese representatives.

00:19:29.940 --> 00:19:32.520
Well then, I’ll announce the results.

00:19:32.520 --> 00:19:35.320
I judge this microwave-bread to have...

00:19:35.320 --> 00:19:38.230
Hey there, wait a moment, please.

00:19:40.030 --> 00:19:40.910
Shachihoko...

00:19:41.780 --> 00:19:43.240
Shachihoko...

00:19:43.240 --> 00:19:45.290
Judge Pierrot.

00:19:45.290 --> 00:19:49.210
I’m saying this to protect America’s honor as well, but...

00:19:50.710 --> 00:19:53.210
That story about the microwave is...

00:19:53.460 --> 00:19:54.710
a lie!

00:19:56.460 --> 00:19:59.130
It’s true that there’s a lot of litigation in America.

00:19:59.130 --> 00:20:02.720
There’s a precedent in which a robber injured himself when the roof fell in while he was breaking in.

00:20:02.720 --> 00:20:07.470
He sued the engineers and occupants of
the house for compensation, and won...

00:20:07.470 --> 00:20:11.730
But, the story of the microwave is just a joke.

00:20:11.730 --> 00:20:14.450
It’s an American joke!

00:20:16.990 --> 00:20:20.910
It’s an urban legend that mocks
people who go too far with their lawsuits.

00:20:20.910 --> 00:20:22.370
An urban legend?

00:20:22.370 --> 00:20:23.540
That’s right.

00:20:24.200 --> 00:20:25.710
R-Really...?

00:20:26.240 --> 00:20:27.950
You know this, right?

00:20:28.790 --> 00:20:33.710
It can’t be that someone like you,
Mr. Pierrot, doesn’t know it’s a joke, right?

00:20:38.130 --> 00:20:40.210
Of course not!

00:20:40.630 --> 00:20:43.600
Man, don’t be taking away a person’s punch line.

00:20:43.600 --> 00:20:44.850
What a pity.

00:20:44.850 --> 00:20:46.220
And, so...

00:20:46.220 --> 00:20:49.720
You pass, Japanese representatives.

00:20:50.350 --> 00:20:51.940
It really was a wonderful bread.

00:20:51.940 --> 00:20:53.740
Really delicious!

00:20:54.860 --> 00:20:57.230
Now look here... you...

00:20:57.230 --> 00:20:59.570
It was a Pierrot-joke!

00:20:59.570 --> 00:21:00.990
Yeah, right.

00:21:01.700 --> 00:21:02.990
You’re dead!

00:21:04.660 --> 00:21:06.950
It was a really delicious bread.

00:21:06.950 --> 00:21:09.920
It was soft, and the sesame seeds on top were great!

00:21:11.750 --> 00:21:13.500
Oh, thank you!

00:21:14.040 --> 00:21:15.750
He ran away.

00:21:15.750 --> 00:21:17.710
He dodged the subject with sesame seeds, eh?

00:21:17.710 --> 00:21:19.380
Good one. You get one zabuton!

00:21:19.380 --> 00:21:21.680
All right, the first match of the
finals begins three days from now.

00:21:21.680 --> 00:21:23.720
Take care!

00:21:23.720 --> 00:21:25.980
Oh, whatever.

00:21:26.560 --> 00:21:27.890
It tastes great.

00:21:27.890 --> 00:21:29.470
We have passed honorably.

00:21:29.470 --> 00:21:31.260
Thank goodness.

00:21:34.190 --> 00:21:37.280
Hey, wait up, Shachihoko.

00:21:38.400 --> 00:21:41.400
Thank you... for helping us today.

00:21:43.280 --> 00:21:45.870
Pierrot’s judging just now was weird.

00:21:45.870 --> 00:21:48.160
I simply did what anyone else would have.

00:21:48.160 --> 00:21:48.910
And...

00:21:48.910 --> 00:21:50.750
Azuma.

00:21:50.750 --> 00:21:54.710
I want to fight with you, fair
and square, to the very end.

00:21:54.710 --> 00:21:55.910
Me, too.

00:21:55.910 --> 00:21:57.290
I’ll tell you this.

00:21:57.290 --> 00:22:01.310
I’ve powered up big time compared to before.

00:22:01.880 --> 00:22:02.970
Moreover...

00:22:02.970 --> 00:22:05.420
I have reliable companions as well.

00:22:05.420 --> 00:22:07.930
I’ll definitely win against you.

00:22:07.930 --> 00:22:10.430
I’m going to win for sure, too.

00:22:11.480 --> 00:22:13.360
Looking forward to it.

00:22:14.600 --> 00:22:15.610
Yeah!

00:24:05.600 --> 00:24:08.960
So, the next match is on
southern island called Tokonatsu.

00:24:08.960 --> 00:24:11.670
It kind of makes you feel like
you’re on a vacation, doesn’t it?

00:24:11.670 --> 00:24:12.970
Look at your surroundings carefully,

00:24:12.970 --> 00:24:14.470
or you might lose your life.

00:24:14.470 --> 00:24:15.360
What was that?!

00:24:15.360 --> 00:24:16.860
Next time, on Yakitate!! Japan,

00:24:16.860 --> 00:24:17.650
Survival!!

00:24:17.650 --> 00:24:19.070
Bakers on an uninhabited island!

00:24:19.070 --> 00:24:19.970
Be sure to watch.

00:24:20.530 --> 00:24:22.730
Yakitate!! Seed of knowledge.

00:24:23.070 --> 00:24:24.320
The very convenient microwave was discovered by

00:24:24.320 --> 00:24:26.810
America’s Professor Spencer, about 60 years ago.

00:24:26.810 --> 00:24:29.480
Microwaves have spread at a rate of higher than 90% in Japan.

00:24:29.480 --> 00:24:31.750
That’s one microwave per household. After all, they’re very convenient.

00:24:32.350 --> 00:24:33.700
Really?!
